---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="What is Varve?" description="Varve is a local-first design suite for vector, layout, typography, motion, prototyping, and print production — all in one cross-platform environment.">
  <div class="container-custom">
    <div class="product-page">
      <h1>What is Varve?</h1>
      <p class="product-intro">
        Varve is a local-first design suite for vector, layout, typography, motion, prototyping, and print production — all in one cross-platform environment. It runs natively on Linux, macOS, and Windows, with a WASM-based web version using the same architecture.
      </p>

      <section class="product-section">
        <h2>Connected creative workflows</h2>
        <p>
          Most creative work spans multiple disciplines. A poster needs vector shapes, typography, and print-ready export. A social media campaign needs layout, animation, and multiple output formats. A product design needs components, prototyping, and developer handoff.
        </p>
        <p>
          Varve is designed to keep this work in one place. Instead of switching between separate applications and rebuilding your context each time, you move between tasks within the same document, the same layer system, and the same interface.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="product-section">
        <h2>Local-first, your files, your machine</h2>
        <p>
          Varve stores all design data locally. No account is required, no cloud sync is needed, and no telemetry is transmitted. You own your files, you control where they live, and you can work offline. The desktop application uses a native Rust engine; the web version uses the same engine compiled to WASM.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="product-section">
        <h2>Cross-platform native performance</h2>
        <p>
          Varve runs natively on Linux (CachyOS, Arch, Ubuntu, Fedora), macOS (Intel and Apple Silicon), and Windows. The rendering architecture separates scene computation from display, using an intermediate representation that replays efficiently to the canvas. The same codebase powers both the native desktop and WASM web builds.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="product-section">
        <h2>Source-available, shaped by its users</h2>
        <p>
          Varve is licensed under FSL-1.1-MIT — free to use, with source code available on GitHub. Development is transparent: architecture decisions, known limitations, and progress are documented publicly. Community input influences priorities. No VC pressure, no feature creep, just focused tools shaped by the people who use them.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="product-section">
        <h2>Who is Varve for?</h2>
        <div class="audience-grid">
          <div class="audience-card">
            <h3>Multi-disciplinary creators</h3>
            <p>Designers, illustrators, and content creators who regularly switch between vector, layout, motion, and print work.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="audience-card">
            <h3>Independent designers</h3>
            <p>Freelancers and small studios who want professional tools without subscription costs or cloud dependencies.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="audience-card">
            <h3>Linux users</h3>
            <p>Creators on Linux who need a native design application with first-class platform support.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="audience-card">
            <h3>Early adopters</h3>
            <p>People who want to test emerging workflows, report problems, and influence the direction of a developing creative tool.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="product-section">
        <h2>Beta status</h2>
        <p>
          Varve is in active beta. Core features are functional and tested, but you may encounter bugs, incomplete tools, or changes to workflows and file formats as development continues. We recommend keeping backups of important work and not relying on Varve as the sole tool for critical production projects at this stage.
        </p>
        <p>
          The value of participating now: test emerging workflows, report problems that matter to you, and help shape where Varve goes next. See the <a href="/support/known-issues">known issues</a> and <a href="/releases">release notes</a> for current limitations.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="product-cta">
        <h2>Try Varve beta</h2>
        <a href="/download" class="btn-primary">Download the beta</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .product-page {
    max-width: 800px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 3rem 0;
  }
  
  .product-page h1 {
    font-size: 2.5rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    color: var(--color-neutral-900);
  }
  
  .product-intro {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    color: var(--color-neutral-600);
    line-height: 1.8;
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }
  
  .product-section {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }
  
  .product-section h2 {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    color: var(--color-neutral-900);
  }
  
  .product-section p {
    color: var(--color-neutral-600);
    line-height: 1.8;
  }
  
  .audience-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(250px, 1fr));
    gap: 1.5rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
  
  .audience-card {
    background: white;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-neutral-200);
    border-radius: 0.75rem;
    padding: 1.5rem;
  }
  
  .audience-card h3 {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--color-neutral-900);
  }
  
  .audience-card p {
    color: var(--color-neutral-600);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  
  .product-cta {
    text-align: center;
    padding: 3rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-neutral-200);
  }
  
  .product-cta h2 {
    font-size: 1.75rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    color: var(--color-neutral-900);
  }
  
  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .product-page h1 {
      font-size: 2rem;
    }
    
    .product-intro {
      font-size: 1rem;
    }
  }
</style>
